Title: S•A•I•H•V (Secure Artificial Intelligence Hyper-Visor) – Agentic Kernel Framework

Overview:
SAIHV is a proof-of-concept for a self-governing, agentic infrastructure kernel. Unlike static automation scripts, SAIHV operates as a State-Aware Environment, continuously monitoring directory entropy and system integrity. It utilizes a recursive Custodian agent to maintain a "Foundry-Clean" system state through policy-driven execution and real-time telemetry.

Core Architecture:

Agentic Logic: Implements an autonomous loop that perceives system state, reasons based on hard-coded safety policies, and acts to remediate deviations.

Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Safety: Incorporates an architectural "Verification Gate" that forces manual human intervention for ambiguous or high-risk system modifications.

Zero-Drift Telemetry: Features a custom verbose reporting protocol ([VERBOSE_LOGIC]) that maps every internal decision-making process to an external output stream for total observability.

Design Philosophy:
This project treats the developer environment as an extension of the system architecture. By prioritizing Minimalist Architectural Hygiene, the SAIHV kernel achieves stability, auditability, and resilience. This framework is designed to bridge the gap between human intent and machine execution, ensuring 1:1 parity between desired system state and reality.

Development Methodology:
The architecture was developed using a "Failure-Driven Empirical" model, where rigorous stress-testing against the kernel's defined constraints was used to harden the logic and optimize performance for edge-compute environments.
